steps.body:
A company leases fiber lines between its six offices, and the carrier quotes a monthly price for each link it could install. Every office must reach every other one, directly or through other offices, for the least total cost. That's a **minimum spanning tree** (MST). A **spanning tree** is a set of edges that connects all n nodes with no cycle, so it has exactly n - 1 edges: an edge that closes a cycle joins two nodes that were connected already and only adds cost. The minimum one has the smallest total weight. When weights tie there can be several, all with the same total.

**Kruskal's algorithm** is greedy. Sort the edges from cheapest to dearest, then take each one unless it closes a cycle. Telling those apart is exactly what `unite` from *Union Find* does: it returns `false` when both ends already share a root.

```cpp harness
struct UnionFind { std::vector<int> parent; std::vector<int> size; explicit UnionFind(int n) : parent(n), size(n, 1) { std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0); } int findRoot(int x) { if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = findRoot(parent[x]); return parent[x]; } bool unite(int a, int b) { int ra = findRoot(a), rb = findRoot(b); if (ra == rb) return false; if (size[ra] < size[rb]) std::swap(ra, rb); parent[rb] = ra; size[ra] += size[rb]; return true; } };
{{code}}
```

```cpp
std::vector<std::tuple<int, int, int>> edges = {{4, 0, 1}, {3, 0, 2}, {1, 1, 2}, {2, 1, 3}, {4, 2, 3}};
std::sort(edges.begin(), edges.end());  // (cost, u, v): cheapest first
UnionFind uf(4);
int total = 0;
for (const auto& [w, u, v] : edges) {
    if (uf.unite(u, v)) {
        total += w;  // it joins two groups: keep it
    }
}
std::cout << total << '\n';  // 6, from the edges 1-2, 1-3, and 0-2
```

Why is greedy safe? Split the nodes into any two sides. The cheapest edge crossing between them belongs in some MST: if an MST leaves it out, adding it closes a cycle, and that cycle crosses the split again at an edge that costs at least as much. Swap the two, and the tree stays connected and costs no more. When Kruskal takes an edge u-v, no cheaper edge leaves u's group, or Kruskal would have reached it first and taken it. So u-v is the cheapest edge across that split, and every edge Kruskal takes is safe.

The sort costs O(m log m) for m edges, and each `unite` is nearly O(1), so the sort dominates. With the cost first in a `std::tuple`, `std::sort` orders by cost and then by the two ends, so ties come out the same on every run.

Stop Kruskal early, when k groups are left, and the groups are k **clusters**: nodes joined by cheap edges end up together. That's single-linkage clustering, the way a researcher groups stocks whose prices move alike.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <numeric>
#include <tuple>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

struct UnionFind {
    std::vector<int> parent;
    std::vector<int> size;

    explicit UnionFind(int n) : parent(n), size(n, 1) {
        std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int findRoot(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = findRoot(parent[x]);
        }
        return parent[x];
    }

    bool unite(int a, int b) {
        int ra = findRoot(a);
        int rb = findRoot(b);
        if (ra == rb) {
            return false;
        }
        if (size[ra] < size[rb]) {
            std::swap(ra, rb);
        }
        parent[rb] = ra;
        size[ra] += size[rb];
        return true;
    }
};

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    int m = 0;
    std::cin >> n >> m;
    std::vector<std::tuple<int, int, int>> edges;  // (cost, u, v)
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int u = 0;
        int v = 0;
        int w = 0;
        std::cin >> u >> v >> w;
        edges.push_back({w, u, v});
    }
    std::sort(edges.begin(), edges.end());  // cheapest first

    UnionFind uf(n);
    long long total = 0;
    int taken = 0;
    for (const auto& [w, u, v] : edges) {
        if (taken == n - 1) {
            break;  // a tree on n nodes has n - 1 edges
        }
        if (uf.unite(u, v)) {
            total += w;
            ++taken;
            std::cout << "take " << u << '-' << v << " cost " << w << '\n';
        } else {
            std::cout << "skip " << u << '-' << v << " cost " << w << '\n';
        }
    }
    if (taken == n - 1) {
        std::cout << "total " << total << " with " << taken << " links" << '\n';
    } else {
        std::cout << "not connected" << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

