steps.body:
A game character, a warehouse robot, or a route planner needs one path, to one goal. Dijkstra finds it, but it spreads out evenly in every direction and settles cells far behind the start before it reaches a goal ahead, because all it knows is g, the cost so far. A smarter search also guesses the cost still to go, a **heuristic** h, and pops the cell with the smallest g + h: its best estimate of a whole path through that cell. That search is A\* (say "A star").

The guess must never overestimate, and a heuristic that never does is **admissible**. Moving up, down, left, and right, with every step costing at least 1, the **Manhattan distance** is admissible: the rows plus the columns left to the goal, since no path gets there in fewer steps. Number the grid's cells `r * cols + c`, and `cell / cols` and `cell % cols` give a cell's row and column back:

```cpp
const int cols = 20;
const int goal = 3 * cols + 17;  // row 3, column 17
auto h = [&](int cell) {
    return std::abs(goal / cols - cell / cols) + std::abs(goal % cols - cell % cols);
};
std::cout << h(3 * cols + 1) << ' ' << h(0) << ' ' << h(goal) << '\n';  // 16 20 0
```

The search is Dijkstra with one change: push `{g + h(v), v}` where Dijkstra pushes `{g, v}`. With h = 0 it is Dijkstra. Manhattan distance is also **consistent**: one step lowers it by at most 1, never more than the step costs. With a consistent h, a cell's cost is final once it pops, as in Dijkstra, so the `done` check stays correct, and the moment the goal pops the search can stop with the cheapest cost. Every consistent h is admissible, but an admissible h that isn't consistent can pop a cell before its cheapest path is found, and then A\* stays exact only if it reopens done cells.

On a grid where every step costs the same, the queue of *Breadth-First Search* already finds the fewest steps. A heuristic would still save it work, but it's when steps cost different amounts that the heap is needed: here, entering mud (`~`) costs 5.

example.code:
#include <climits>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

using Item = std::pair<int, int>;  // (priority, cell)

const int DR[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
const int DC[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

// The cheapest path from start to goal, with cells numbered r * cols + c. Entering mud (~)
// costs 5 and anything else 1. Marks each cell it explores with 'o'.
void search(std::vector<std::string>& grid, int start, int goal, bool astar) {
    const int rows = static_cast<int>(grid.size());
    const int cols = static_cast<int>(grid[0].size());
    auto h = [&](int cell) {  // Manhattan distance to the goal for A*, 0 for Dijkstra
        return astar ? std::abs(goal / cols - cell / cols) + std::abs(goal % cols - cell % cols) : 0;
    };
    std::vector<int> dist(rows * cols, INT_MAX);
    std::vector<bool> done(rows * cols, false);
    std::priority_queue<Item, std::vector<Item>, std::greater<Item>> pq;
    dist[start] = 0;
    pq.push({h(start), start});
    int expanded = 0;
    while (!pq.empty()) {
        const int u = pq.top().second;
        pq.pop();
        if (done[u]) {
            continue;  // stale entry
        }
        done[u] = true;
        ++expanded;
        if (u == goal) {
            break;  // its cost is final once it pops
        }
        if (grid[u / cols][u % cols] == '.') {
            grid[u / cols][u % cols] = 'o';
        }
        for (int d = 0; d < 4; ++d) {
            const int nr = u / cols + DR[d];
            const int nc = u % cols + DC[d];
            if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols || grid[nr][nc] == '#') {
                continue;
            }
            const int v = nr * cols + nc;
            const int g = dist[u] + (grid[nr][nc] == '~' ? 5 : 1);
            if (g < dist[v]) {
                dist[v] = g;
                pq.push({g + h(v), v});  // Dijkstra's priority is g alone
            }
        }
    }
    std::cout << (astar ? "A*" : "Dijkstra") << ": cost " << dist[goal] << ", expanded " << expanded << '\n';
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::string> map = {
        "....................",
        "......~~~~~~........",
        "......~~~~~~...#....",
        ".S....~~~~~~...#.G..",
        "......~~~~~~...#....",
        "......~~~~~~........",
        "....................",
    };
    const int cols = static_cast<int>(map[0].size());
    for (bool astar : {false, true}) {
        std::vector<std::string> grid = map;
        search(grid, 3 * cols + 1, 3 * cols + 17, astar);  // S at row 3, column 1, G at column 17
        for (const std::string& row : grid) {
            std::cout << row << '\n';
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

